igned symmetry and constitutive freedom.\n- Sections 6; PDF pages 12-15: Gluing contracts, copied versus unique carriers, dynamic memory, conditioning.\n- Sections 7-8; PDF pages 15-18: Prepared assembly, shape comparisons, full-state persistence versus readout access.\n- Sections 9; PDF pages 18-20: Projective receipts, covariance and history information.\n- Sections 10-12; PDF pages 20-24: Prepared apparatus, finite capture, renewal, resources and physical bridge.\n- Sections 13; PDF pages 24-25: Native event-source boundaries and joint-state obligations.\n- Sections A-C; PDF pages 25-27: Source crosswalk, comparison contracts and review provenance.\n- Sections D.1-D.8; PDF pages 27-32: Flow/closure proofs, refined joint error, representation and stacked memory.\n- Sections D.9-D.11; PDF pages 32-40: Conditioning, sampling, metric-scoped transfer and supplied channels.\n- Sections D.12-D.14; PDF pages 40-46: Receipt alphabet, history graph, kernel freedom, winding and affine control.\n- Sections D.15; PDF pages 46-48: Passive hysteresis candidate, finite activity, noise and symmetry collision.\n- Sections D.16; PDF pages 49-51: Sampled guards, event-time intervals, precision/cadence and storage.\n- Sections D.17; PDF pages 51-52: Receipt consistency, correlated error covariance and blind error subspace.\n- Sections E and references; PDF pages 53-56: Reported companion packages, development disclosures and source references.\n\n## High-value source claims retained for later work\n\n- Section 6.5 / D.8: the stated stacked-family theorem concerns independent copied cell dynamics, fresh-vertex face attachments, all three nonzero constant exchange weights, arbitrary copied initial states and an exact autonomous linear realization retaining the normalized visible coordinates. It states n=15t, m=8t+7, rank(Q;QA)=15t and additional state dimension 7(t-1). This is not the native unique-simplex generator or a count of physical memory bits.\n- Section 6.6 / D.9: exact identi