iguration selec-\ntion nor a calibrated physical metric, universal entropy-area density or gravitational equation.\nKeywords: tetrahedral complexes; incidence dynamics; persistence; projective reconstruction;\nfinite memory; capture; renewal.\nSource and scope. The technical baseline is the recovered four-paper M5 release dated 4\nOctober 2026: Papers I–III review drafts 1.1.1 and Paper IV development draft 0.1 [1–4]. This\nis a separate synthesis manuscript. The four source papers retain their identities and theorem\nnumbering. The first closure package reconciles the four paper bytes against repository commit\n4b9c1f7ba03f76324b792f389517161d2bc47e05 and integrates selected later source/interface\nresults. It is not a complete audit of every subsequent development. All results retain their\nstated assumptions. Review candidate; no independent review or registry promotion is claimed.\nPhysical promotion: 0.\n\n\n1. The problem: how does change produce things that last?\nThe world changes, yet it contains things we can recognize again. Signals travel, structures\nsurvive encounters, and interactions leave traces. A theoretical account should explain how\nthese processes connect: how a structure is generated, how it preserves identifiable properties,\nand how its behaviour becomes available to another process.\nThese are related questions with different mathematical requirements. Existence in a state space\nis not a formation mechanism. Conservation of a full-state quantity is not guaranteed access\nthrough a restricted readout. A successful transfer is not permanent memory. The purpose of\nthis manuscript is to make those distinctions part of one explanatory argument.\nThe organizing question is:\n      Can constrained interactions explain the formation of persistent structures and read-\n      able records within a common dynamical framework?\nUD investigates tetrahedral packet complexes as a candidate architecture for that framework.\nThe word packet denotes a declared carrier and its state; it does not independently establish a\nparticle, a material substance or an isolated unit of physical energy. The theory must earn each\nof those interpretations through a further map to observables.\n\n                                                 3\n\fThe approach begins with a finite setting because its relationships and consequences can be\ninspected exactly. A small carrier permits an explicit boundary matrix, an exact spectrum, a\ncomplete observation map and a finite apparatus state. Larger claims should remain accountable\nto these constructions.\nOur narrative progresses through five questions. What can differ? What can interact? What\nsurvives that interaction? What can a readout recover? What mechanism preserves the recov-\nered difference as a record? Formation and the physical bridge then become precise obligations\nrather than unexplained transitions between equations.\n\n1.1 Object, representation and observation\nUD distinguishes the system being described from the mathematical model, numerical encoding,\nand observation used to describe it. An exact expression and a finite approximation may refer\nto the same intended quantity while carrying different information. Likewise, a complete state\nand a restricted readout can support different conclusions about distinguishability.\nThe programme therefore asks which relationships survive changes of representation, which\ndistinctions remain accessible after coarse-graining, and what mechanisms make that access\npersistent. Numerical convenience is a methodological choice whose consequences must be ac-\ncounted for. Continuity, discreteness, dimensionality, and scale invariance remain properties to\nbe established within a justified physical description.\nThis is the philosophical role of distinction in the present account: it makes access and persis-\ntence explicit without equating the description with its physical referent. The methodological\ngovernance addition [10] operationalizes this separation through exact specifications, transported\nobservables, and declared approximation errors.\n\n1.2 Exact representation and controlled approximation\n                                                                                                 √\nThe two-tetrahedron model in §6.1 has a squared frequency 3, hence the exact frequency 3,\ndefined by 𝑥2 = 3 and 𝑥 > 0. This is a model-derived example, not a universal physical constant.\nIt is irrational: if 𝑥 = 𝑎/𝑏 in lowest terms, 𝑎2 = 3𝑏2 gives an even exponent of the prime 3 on\nthe left and an odd exponent on the right, a contradiction. Its nonterminating, non-eventually-\nperiodic expansion occurs in every integer positional base. A finite expansion is rational, as is an\neventually periodic expansion by a geometric-series sum. Thus changing numeral base cannot\nmake this irrational quantity a finite fraction.\n                                                                           √                     √\nA radical must also retain its declared meaning. For example, √4/3 = 2/ 3 is distinct from 2;\ntaking the square root of a supplied ratio does not derive a carrier frequency or physical law. A\nnumerical match between quantities with different definitions requires an explicit correspondence.\nAn exact expression, including 1/3 = 0.3, need not discard information. A finite truncation is a\ndifferent object. The reduced rational 𝑎/𝑏 terminates in base 𝐵 precisely when 𝑏 divides 𝐵𝑛 for\nsome 𝑛, equivalently when all denominator primes divide 𝐵. This is an arithmetic property of\nencoding. It establishes no physical fractality or fundamental discreteness.\nFor an invertible coordinate change 𝑦 = 𝑇 𝑥, equivalent dynamics and observations obey\n\n\n                             𝐴𝑦 = 𝑇 𝐴𝑥 𝑇 −1 ,     𝑂𝑦 (𝑦) = 𝑂𝑥 (𝑇 −1 𝑦).\n\nIf squared norm is 𝑥𝑇 𝐻𝑥 𝑥, its metric transforms as\n\n\n                                      𝐻𝑦 = (𝑇 −1 )𝑇 𝐻𝑥 𝑇 −1 .\n\n\n\n                                                 4\n\fTransporting the state while keeping an incompatible generator, metric or noise model changes\nthe calculation. An information-losing projection is another operation: it needs a closure crite-\nrion and an account of the distinctions it forgets.\n                                                                              √        √\nThis discipline has a direct application to recurrence. The exact frequencies 3 and 5 have\nno positive common period. If operative frequencies are instead replaced by the finite decimals\n\n                                           433                   559\n                                 1.732 =       ,       2.236 =       ,\n                                           250                   250\n\nthe rounded-frequency model has a common period 500𝜋, since its phase advances are 2𝜋⋅433 and\n2𝜋 ⋅ 559. Display rounding alone creates no such change when the exact quantities are retained\ninternally. The artefact arises when the approximations are promoted into the dynamics.\nThe example does not prohibit numerical modelling. It requires the numerical model’s error and\nintended domain to accompany its predictions. Exact algebra also remains conditional on the\nchosen model; it cannot establish the physical interpretation of that model by itself.\n\n1.3 Scope, contribution and relation to prior work\nThe paper studies finite models of distinction, access and retention. Its reviewable claim is that\nthese are separate mathematical obligations, illustrated by concrete tetrahedral calculations. It\ndoes not establish that tetrahedra are the unique architecture of nature. The narrative motivates\nthe models; the declared assumptions determine their consequences.\nCombinatorial Laplacians on simplicial complexes are established objects [11]. Discrete Dirac\nconstructions likewise predate this manuscript [12]. Knill gives the complete-graph Dirac spec-\ntrum, including the filled tetrahedron [18, §9.3]. The phase equivalence in