tion when 𝑂(𝑥) ≠ 𝑂(𝑦). Under a noisy or finite-resolution\nobservation, distinguishability additionally depends on the output distributions, permitted de-\ncisions and error criterion.\nThis operational definition makes the model’s alternatives explicit. It does not imply that phys-\nical existence depends on observation, nor that every state difference is already an irreversible\nrecord. Distinction under a readout is relative to a specified map.\nFor real chain states, the observation might retain signed coordinates or only their squares.\nFor complex states, it might retain amplitudes, phases or bilinear receipts. These alternatives\npreserve different information. A narrative that calls them all “information” without naming\nthe map would conceal the central problem.\n\n\n\n                                               6\n\f2.2 A finite binary label register\nOn four vertices, the tetrahedral edge graph has three opposite-edge matchings:\n\n\n                       𝑀1 = {01, 23},    𝑀2 = {02, 13},    𝑀3 = {03, 12}.\n\nAssigning one bit to each defines Θ = 𝔽32 . Its eight labels separate into Hamming-weight sectors\nof dimensions 1, 3, 3, 1. Vertex permutations induce an onto action 𝑆4 → 𝑆3 on the matchings,\nwith Klein-four kernel [1, §3].\nThe labeled product set 𝑆4 × Θ has 24 ⋅ 8 = 192 elements. Under the specified reference-edge\nprojection, its six edge fibers each have 32 elements. These are exact counts of declared objects.\nThey do not establish a physical coupling, a 32-stage history or a clock period. On the same\nfiber one can supply an identity update, a transposition or a 32-cycle.\nThe binary register and the amplitude carrier below therefore have different roles. The first\norganizes labels; the second supports continuous state evolution. A source law relating them\nmust be supplied or derived. Counting their coordinates does not provide that law.\n\n2.3 Comparing event labels and composing a history\nA receipt alphabet can distinguish event types without specifying which events can follow each\nother. The additional working notes [24] motivate a conditional 44-label alphabet: eight fixed-\nweight labels, eighteen onset labels and eighteen extinction labels, including address and parity.\nIt has 946 unordered comparisons of distinct labels. Only 400 pairs have even one ordering with\ncompatible incoming and outgoing Hamming weights. Full bit, frame and transport rules may\nreduce this set further. These counts organize possible comparisons; an admissible event graph\nand its update kernel remain separate obligations.\nThe onset/extinction block illustrates why numerical matches need typed provenance. Both\nits same-address subset and its reverse-weight-step subset have 108 pairs, yet their intersection\ncontains only 36. They are different subsets. Neither their count ratio nor an assigned address\ncost determines a winding increment. Appendix D.12 derives the counts and shows that a\nwinding or cost is recoverable from a receipt precisely when it is constant on each receipt fiber.\n\n2.4 A reference kernel exposes what counting cannot select\nAppendix D.13 supplies a declared fine-history model for that alphabet: a permutation of the\nthree pair addresses followed by no flip or one addressed flip. Its eight Boolean vertices admit\n192 action-distinct edges. Separating the identity/no-flip null gives 44 active receipt labels plus\none null symbol. This all-open graph realizes every weight-compatible active receipt word, but\nits admission rule is supplied rather than derived from the continuous carrier.\nA complete normalized kernel can be written on this graph. Uniform primitive controls give\nstationary weight populations (1, 3, 3, 1)/8. Other kernels with the same permutation symmetry\nand positive action support realize every positive stationary weight distribution. Thus a valid\nevent alphabet and its symmetry do not determine formation preferences. The kernel’s source\nand physical clock remain additional obligations; this is a prepared classical model, not the skew\nflow of §4 [25].\n\n2.5 When does continuous change count as an event?\nA changing stock does not name its own event. A response rule must specify which change\nmatters and what persists after it. Appendix D.15 tests one explicit candidate: each opposite-\nedge stock drives a memory bit with distinct on and off thresholds. Once those thresholds\n\n                                                7\n\fand an initial memory are supplied, the carrier trajectory determines a unique sequence of\nsimultaneous update batches. The gap between thresholds gives finite activity and a bound that\nsurvives specified observation error.\nThe construction also reveals a constraint on the narrative. A symmetric vertex preparation\ncan drive all three pair addresses across a threshold together. Choosing exactly one address\nthen requires an additional reference, asymmetric state or stochastic mechanism; the coherent\ntrajectory alone does not distinguish one. The natural covariant batch is outside the reference\ngraph’s one-flip primitive alphabet, although its endpoint belongs to the composition group. This\ncandidate connects a carrier signal to explicit event semantics without deriving that semantics,\na native geometry update or a free physical measurement [27].\nReading an event requires another contract. Finite-rate threshold polling can miss a guard touch\neven when the ideal response has a positive dwell time. Appendix D.16 supplies conservative\nsampled guards and an explicit excursion/persistence condition under which every address-wise\nevent is confirmed within a bounded delay. Cross-address order needs additional timing sepa-\nration or uncertainty receipts: one simultaneous carrier batch can split across several detection\nticks. The distinction matters before a recorded order is used to compute a history-dependent\nquantity [29].\n\n\n3. Why tetrahedral structure enters\n3.1 Relationships before metric\n