ation are supplied here for review. Appendix D.7\nproves the sector and character statements.\nThe dimensions above require full 𝑆4 . Restricting to proper tetrahedral rotations 𝐴4 identifies\nthe two three-dimensional types and trivializes the sign type. The full carrier becomes 31 ⊕ 43,\nso its real commutant dimension is 25 and its skew dimension is 9. One cannot replace 𝑆4 by 𝐴4\nwhile retaining the 13/3 counts. The coeﬀicient field is also part of the model. Auxiliary finite-\nfield modules require a declared action and an observable bridge before they can describe the\nreal carrier. For example, the additional notes correctly exclude a surjection from the order-26\ngroup 𝐷13 onto 𝑆3 ; an extension by the order-four Klein group has order 104 and still cannot\nsupply that quotient. Valid arithmetic on a chosen group or field does not select it as the native\nframe symmetry [24].\n\n5.2 Quadratic context and its limits\nThe quadratic carrier also matters. For the standard three-dimensional representation,\n\n                               2\n                           Sym0 (𝑇2 ) = 𝐸 ⊕ 𝑇2 ,        Hom𝑆4 (𝐸, 𝑇2 ) = 0.\n\nThe two sectors share a quadratic parent but admit no nonzero equivariant linear identification.\nA context permitting 𝑅 ⊗ 𝐸 → 𝑇2 needs 𝑅 = 𝑇1 or 𝑇2 among irreducible contexts; its minimum\ndimension is three [3, §10]. Each permitted irreducible context has a one-dimensional intertwiner\nspace. A three-dimensional carrier of type 𝐴1 ⊕𝐸 fails this criterion despite having the minimum\nnumerical dimension. The representation type, not dimension alone, makes the channel available.\nThe source constructs a compatible tensor basis, while its scalar strength remains a separate\nchoice.\nSymmetry therefore answers which interactions are possible and which are excluded. A constitu-\ntive rule must answer how strongly the permitted interactions occur. A finite symmetry group\nis not, by itself, a derived physical gauge group.\n\n\n\n\n                      