are\nexported to donor and history. They are not destroyed. Repetition consumes fresh donor and\noutput slots, and failures consume resources too.\nOn matching full joint domains, admitted bank error 𝜖𝐹 and operation comparisons 𝜖𝑗 compose\nunconditionally as\n\n\n                                  𝜖total ≤ min{1, 𝜖𝐹 + ∑ 𝜖𝑗 }.\n                                                        𝑗\n\n\nThis requires uniform operation bounds on the admitted joint states. Isolated-input results do\nnot automatically meet that requirement. The bank error is charged once; diagnostics already\ncovered by it are not charged again as independent errors.\n\n11.2 Coherent supply is a resource\nThe source constructs a uniform reference packet of length 𝐿 = 2𝑞 from a seed and supplied\ncoherent binary fuel [4, Theorem IV.2]. Here Δ > 0, fuel qubit 𝑗 has Hamiltonian Δ2𝑗 |1⟩⟨1|\nand state |+⟩, and the independent reference begins at seed |𝑛0 ⟩ on a suﬀiciently long finite\nladder. The seed energy 𝑛0 Δ is additional to fuel energy. Matched disjoint exchanges transfer\nthe supplied coherence into the reference. Ideal independent fuel has\n\n\n                      ⟨𝐻𝐹 ⟩ = Δ(𝐿 − 1)/2,       Var 𝐻𝐹 = Δ2 (𝐿2 − 1)/12.\n\nThe exchanges conserve the declared sum of bare energies while transferring the supplied coher-\nence. They do not create that coherence from an unspecified reservoir.\nFor a complete stationary input processed covariantly under the declared additive free-energy\ngenerators, the reference remains stationary. With nondegenerate packet energies, its distance\n\n                                               22\n\ffrom the pure uniform packet is at least 1 − 1/𝐿 [4, Theorem IV.3]. Nonstationary supply can\nevade that particular obstruction, but must enter the ledger.\n\n11.3 What a complete cost comparison includes\nCost requires declared energy operators, initial states, interaction and switching conventions,\nclock preparation, write and readout, retention, renewal, and finite exhau