e jitter 𝑗𝑠 , use at most\nΔnominal + 2𝑗𝑠 instead.\nEven an ideal simultaneous batch may split into several detection batches under address-wise\nerror. Such a log records acquisition order. It must not be used as a fine carrier-event order\nwithout further evidence. If 𝑚! different serial orders remain possible and the desired functional\ndistinguishes all of them, recovering a realized order requires at least ⌈log2 (𝑚!)⌉ additional fixed-\nlength bits, conditional on the known event set. This is a necessary storage condition, not a\nmechanism for observing the order; guessing those bits adds no evidence. True batches and\nunresolved order remain valid receipt objects.\n\nD.16.4 Quantization, an exact fixture and a finite storage ledger\nA 𝑏-bit uniform quantizer on [0, 𝑅2 ] with bin-midpoint outputs has maximum quantization error\n𝑅2 /2𝑏+1 . Clipping an analog reading to that interval cannot increase its error relative to a true\nstock in the interval. With analog error at most 𝜀𝑎 , use\n\n\n                                         𝜀 = 𝜀𝑎 + 𝑅2 /2𝑏+1 .\n\nA suﬀicient design inequality for the delay certificate is\n\n\n                              2𝜀𝑎 + 𝑅2 /2𝑏 < 𝜈(𝑑∗ − Δ),          Δ < 𝑑∗ .\n\nThe excursion hypotheses must still be checked. These are logical precision and cadence budgets,\nnot an ADC implementation or a physical energy law.\nFor the canonical unit vertex trajectory, use 𝑅 = 1, ℓ = 1/16, ℎ = 3/16, 𝑏 = 6, 𝜀𝑎 = 0\nand\n√ Δ = 1/64. Then 𝜀 = 1/128. On the required rising/falling bands, the speed is at least\n  39/16 > 3/8; choose 𝜈 = 3/8. The confirmation core has length at least 3/64 > Δ: its stock\nmargin to the peak or trough is 3/64 and |𝑞𝑝′ | ≤ 2. The sinusoidal upper and lower cores are\neach a single interval. Thus\n\n                                   2(1/128)   1    11     1\n                              𝐷=            +    =     <    ≤ 𝑑∗ .\n                                     3/8      64   192   16\n\nSix bits are the minimum for the chosen 