n, physical implementation, complete energy cost and\nentropy per boundary area remain open.\n\nD.17 Receipt consistency, covariance and errors it cannot reveal\nD.17.1 The declared signless tree map\nThe filled tetrahedron has a Hasse graph with 15 simplex vertices and 28 adjacent-degree con-\ntainment edges. Choose the 14-edge spanning tree and 14 chords specified in the certificate [30].\nFor a declared latent field 𝑧 ∈ ℝ15 , let 𝑆, 𝐻 ∈ {0, 1}14×15 map 𝑧 to endpoint sums on tree and\nchord edges. These are signless observation matrices, not the signed boundary operator or skew\ngenerator.\nThe graph is bipartite by simplex-degree parity. Let 𝑣 have value −1 on even degrees and +1\non odd degrees. Then 𝑆𝑣 = 𝐻𝑣 = 0. Connectivity gives ker 𝑆 = span{𝑣}, so every chord row\nis in the tree row space. Along its odd-length tree path, alternating the endpoint sums cancels\ninternal vertices and retains the two endpoints. Hence\n\n\n                                  𝐻 = 𝐶𝑆,        𝐶 ∈ {−1, 0, 1}14×14 .\n\nFor the supplied tree, rank 𝑆 = 14 and rank 𝐻 = rank 𝐶 = 10. The longest chord path has\nseven edges. A different tree can change 𝐶 and its error amplification. The graph argument is\nstandard incidence/path algebra; the certificate identifies this particular split, not a new general\nreconstruction method.\nA possible magnitude-receipt application requires a separate map. On full nonzero support,\nif a product receipt is |𝐽𝑖𝑗 | = 2|𝐵𝑖𝑗 ||𝑐𝑖 ||𝑐𝑗 |, define 𝑧𝑖 = log |𝑐𝑖 | and 𝑦𝑖𝑗 = log(|𝐽𝑖𝑗 |/(2|𝐵𝑖𝑗 |)).\nThen 𝑦𝑖𝑗 = 𝑧𝑖 + 𝑧𝑗 . Zero amplitudes, phase/sign information and calibration require additional\ntreatment. Applying these signless matrices directly to the chain amplitudes instead defines\nanother observable. The null direction 𝑣 is a measurement gauge: 𝐴𝑣 ≠ 0 for the native\ncanonical generator, so it is not a harmonic carrier mode.\n\nD.17.2 What zero residual covariance establishes\nFor noiseless observations 𝑌𝑆 = 𝑆𝑧 and 𝑌𝐻 = 𝐻𝑧,\n\n\n                                